WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.941
Audit logs and reversibility.

2
00:00:02.221 --> 00:00:09.347
When something goes wrong, you need to answer three questions fast: what did the agent do, why was it allowed, and can we undo it?

3
00:00:09.627 --> 00:00:11.739
An audit log answers the first two.

4
00:00:12.019 --> 00:00:28.744
OWASP’s logging cheat sheet recommends logging security-relevant events such as authorization failures and high-risk actions, with enough context (when, where, who, what) to reconstruct them, and it warns against writing secrets and sensitive personal data into logs.

5
00:00:29.024 --> 00:00:46.667
For an agent, write one record per tool call, including the blocked ones: the run and step, the tool and its exact arguments (redacting secrets), the decision (allow, ask, deny), the rule that decided it, whether a human approved and who, whether it executed, and the result or error.

6
00:00:46.947 --> 00:00:53.240
Add the model id, prompt version, and token counts so you can connect behavior to configuration.

7
00:00:53.520 --> 00:01:02.117
Blocked calls are the most valuable rows: they show what the agent tried to do, which is your early warning that something in its inputs is steering it.

8
00:01:02.397 --> 00:01:10.248
The Managed Agents guide suggests going further with an optional offline reviewer that reads every finished transcript on a schedule.

9
00:01:10.528 --> 00:01:12.448
Reversibility is the other half.

10
00:01:12.728 --> 00:01:20.472
For each write tool, decide before launch how you would undo it: delete the draft, revert the commit, close the issue, post a correction.

11
00:01:20.752 --> 00:01:27.045
If the honest answer is “we can’t,” that tool belongs behind a human, or out of the agent entirely.

12
00:01:27.325 --> 00:01:30.675
Write the undo procedure into your runbook, and test it once.

13
00:01:30.955 --> 00:01:35.819
A log tells you what happened; a tested undo path is what makes an incident small.
